{"qid":"Q190165","names":["Kingdom of Luang Phrabang"],"title":"Kingdom of Luang Phrabang","endonym":{"native":"ຫຼວງ ພະບາງ","lang":"lo"},"capsule":"The Kingdom of Luang Phrabang (ຫຼວງ ພະບາງ) was established in 1707 when the Lan Xang confederation split after dynastic succession disputes, leaving Luang Phrabang as the northernmost successor state controlling the upper Mekong valley. The kingdom maintained a tributary relationship with China, Siam, and eventually Vietnam, navigating competing pressures through careful diplomacy while preserving its Theravada Buddhist culture and royal institutions centered on the Phabang image. French protectorate status was imposed in 1893 following Siamese recognition of French suzerainty under the Franco-Siamese Treaty; the royal house continued as a protectorate within French Indochina until Laos's independence in 1953, though the monarchy — and Luang Phrabang's royal lineage — was abolished only in 1975.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The kingdom's core territory along the upper Mekong is documented; precise highland frontiers separating it from Vientiane and Vietnamese-influenced zones were porous and not surveyed with cartographic precision until the French colonial period.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of Luang Phrabang","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Luang_Phrabang"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Lan Xang","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lan_Xang"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Franco-Siamese Treaty of 1893","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franco-Siamese_Treaty_of_1893"}],"britannica":"place/Louangphrabang","cowCode":null,"wiki